<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jing Ke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 227 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jing Ke was a retainer of Crown Prince Dan of Yan who tried to assassinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">King Zheng of Qin</a>, the future First Emperor, in 227 BCE. Admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> court with a map of Yan territory hiding a dagger and the head of a Qin defector, he failed and was killed. Qin answered by invading Yan, and Jing Ke became the model of the self-sacrificing assassin in Chinese tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jing-ke.jpg" alt="Jing Ke" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ReijiYamashina777 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Ke_assassination_attempt_Wu_Family_Shrine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
